<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Platform-neutral half of VibeSuperTonic. Shared by the Windows SAPI engine
    and (from Phase 3) the Linux daemon — see docs/LINUX-PORT-PLAN.md.

    THREE RULES, all load-bearing:

      1. TargetFramework is net10.0, NOT net10.0-windows. Windows-only types are
         still *reachable* from a neutral TFM but are flagged by CA1416, which
         VstPortable promotes to an error. That is the enforcement — a
         `using Microsoft.Win32;` in here fails the build on the machine that
         wrote it, not on the other platform's CI job days later.

      2. NO PackageReference. Ever. The DirectML and CPU builds of ONNX Runtime
         ship the same managed assembly name with different managed API surfaces
         (R-13 in the port plan), so an assembly shared by both hosts cannot
         reference either one. Core stays package-free and the ORT-touching code
         lives in the per-platform backends. If something here seems to need a
         package, it belongs in a host project instead.

      3. No InternalsVisibleTo. The end state (Way 3) compiles these sources
         directly into each host rather than referencing a built assembly, where
         `internal` becomes internal-to-each-host and cross-assembly grants stop
         meaning anything. Everything the tests need is public on purpose, so
         that migration is a csproj edit rather than a refactor.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <AssemblyName>VibeSuperTonic.Core</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>VibeSuperTonic.Core</RootNamespace>
    <VstPortable>true</VstPortable>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
